{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time dilation time dilation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","fileName":"Time Dilation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Dilation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-17 00:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Gravitation and Relativity]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Gravitation and Relativity","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time dilation time dilation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","fileName":"Time Dilation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Dilation","heading":"Time Dilation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"In special relativity, a clock moving relative to an inertial observer is measured to run slowly. If $\\Delta\\tau$ is the proper time recorded with the events at one location, then $\\Delta t=\\gamma\\Delta\\tau$, where $\\gamma=1/\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}$. The effect is reciprocal between inertial frames and becomes large near light speed.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Gravitation and Relativity","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time dilation time dilation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Dilation.md","fileName":"Time Dilation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Dilation","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Gravitation and Relativity","f